WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
Decide what “better” means before you start.

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:16.451
Anthropic’s prompt engineering overview opens with a precondition, not a technique: before you engineer a prompt, have a clear definition of success for your use case, some way to test against it empirically, and a first draft.

3
00:00:16.731 --> 00:00:26.693
Its guide to success criteria asks that criteria be specific, measurable, achievable (realistic for current models), and relevant to what users need.

4
00:00:26.973 --> 00:00:31.389
It also notes that most uses need several criteria at once, not one.

5
00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:34.891
“The summaries should be good” fails every one of those tests.

6
00:00:35.171 --> 00:00:52.131
Compare: “On our 50 test status updates, at least 95% of summaries name every milestone whose date changed, none contain a date absent from the source, 100% parse as our JSON schema, and the median response time stays under 4 seconds.

7
00:00:52.411 --> 00:00:56.336
” Now you know what to measure, and so does anyone reviewing your change.

8
00:00:56.616 --> 00:00:59.368
Then build the test set before you touch the prompt.

9
00:00:59.648 --> 00:01:04.021
Anthropic’s eval guide gives three design principles that fit this stage.

10
00:01:04.301 --> 00:01:18.595
Make tests task-specific, mirroring your real input distribution, including edge cases: irrelevant or missing input, overly long input, poor or harmful input, and ambiguous cases where even people would disagree.

11
00:01:18.875 --> 00:01:22.565
Automate grading where you can (exact match, string checks, code).

12
00:01:22.845 --> 00:01:28.029
And prefer more test cases with automated checks over a few cases graded by hand.

13
00:01:28.309 --> 00:01:44.053
Hamel Husain’s widely read essay on evals makes the same argument from practice: start with cheap assertion-style tests that run on every change, the way unit tests do, and save expensive human and model-based review for a regular cadence.
